  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday morning through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 209 PM EDT Thu Jul 2 2026\n\nIndependence Day is poised to be unseasonably hot and mostly dry,\nas the ridging that has supported our multi-day heat wave begins \nto break down. Isolated thunderstorms are possible -- primarily \nacross far northern and western Georgia along the Alabama state \nline -- as moisture begins to intrude upon the deteriorating heat \ndome, but coverage is unlikely to provide much (if any) relief \nfrom the lingering heat. Confidence in potentially dangerous heat \nremains high, and a Heat Advisory has been proactively issued to \ncarry through Saturday evening. Highs areawide will be in the mid-\nto-upper 90s, with heat index values in the triple digits for all\nbut the terrain of far northeast Georgia. Outdoor activities are \na hallmark of the holiday weekend, and with continued oppressive \nheat, precautions must be taken to lessen likelihood of heated- \nrelated illnesses. Ensure that you are taking frequent hydration \nbreaks (in the shade if at all possible), wearing loose, light- \ncolored clothing, and checking in on your people. Apparent/\"feels \nlike\" temperatures will cross the 90 degree mark by 10AM, and will\nlikely not cross below that threshold until 11PM, meaning it may \nstill be uncomfortably hot even after the sun goes down. \n\nSunday and beyond, mid-level flow becomes more nebulous, with the \nSoutheast broadly situated with the base of troughing to the north \nand ridging across the Gulf to the south. At the surface, the \nwestern periphery of the Bermuda High nudges onshore, allowing \ndewpoints to surge back into the low-to-mid 70s with moist \nsouthwesterly flow off of the Gulf. Chances for afternoon showers \nand thunderstorms will be more numerous through midweek, owing to \nabundant moisture and lack of intervening/inhibiting subsidence as \nin previous days. Increased cloud cover correlated to higher PoPs \nwill support air temperatures just a tick lower each day -- \ngenerally in the low-to-mid 90s -- however, higher dewpoints will \nkeep heat index values in the triple digits for most through the end \nof the extended.",
